/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS - DIAtracer/par_review_codex2.out:2105:- **Peak RSS may increase substantially during Phase A.** `MassTraceDetection::run()` copies qualifying spectra and peaks into its local `work_exp`. With several windows processed concurrently, memory includes all original window maps, multiple active `work_exp` copies, `MassTrace` results, and retained `WindowFrags`. Clearing each source map only *after* detection cannot reduce the transient detection peak. Given the existing 72 GB RSS, this needs measurement and probably concurrency throttling; otherwise the parallel version may simply run out of memory.
/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS - DIAtracer/par_review_codex2.out:2172:- **Peak RSS may increase substantially during Phase A.** `MassTraceDetection::run()` copies qualifying spectra and peaks into its local `work_exp`. With several windows processed concurrently, memory includes all original window maps, multiple active `work_exp` copies, `MassTrace` results, and retained `WindowFrags`. Clearing each source map only *after* detection cannot reduce the transient detection peak. Given the existing 72 GB RSS, this needs measurement and probably concurrency throttling; otherwise the parallel version may simply run out of memory.
/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS - DIAtracer/par_code_review_kimi.out:317:  But wait - we should double-check that `detectTraces_` doesn't store references. Let's look at `MassTraceDetection::run`. It takes `const PeakMap&` and produces `vector<MassTrace>`. MassTrace objects in OpenMS contain a vector of `MassTracePeak` (or `Peak2D` copies). Each MassTracePeak stores the actual data. So no references. The `toTrace` function copies everything into `Trace`. So safe.
/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS - DIAtracer/par_code_review_kimi.out:327:  21. **`MassTraceDetection::setLogType(ProgressLogger::NONE)`**: Called inside `detectTraces_`, which is serial. Good. If it were called in parallel, the `setLogType` on a shared object could race. But it's serial.
