/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS - DIAtracer/DIA-tools-vault/xic/ion-mobility-xic.md:39:`min_sample_rate` 0.5 chains any point within ppm tolerance, so two co-eluting species at the same m/z (routine in a wide diaPASEF isolation window) fuse into one elongated trace with a single wrong apex. Two measurements point here: (i) relaxing `min_sample_rate` 0.5→0.1 gained 9 fragments but **lost 130 peptides** — the signature of longer, more-merged traces with wrong apexes/shapes; (ii) our Pearson has degenerated into a co-location measure, which a merged trace corrupts maximally, since it co-locates with everything under it. Splitting restores per-species apexes, making the +/-3 s apex gate and any shape score meaningful, and improves precision without cutting fragment counts — the one direction the falsification table leaves open. See [[zero-padded-pearson-defect]].
/Users/kohlbach/Claude/OpenMS - DIAtracer/DIA-tools-vault/xic/ion-mobility-xic.md:42:73.6% of missing peptides are absent at *any* score; that absent set is 65% charge 2+. Splitting merged MS2 traces cannot make an absent precursor appear — it only re-attributes fragments among precursors already detected. Only MS1 splitting adds precursors, and MS1 is where merging is rarest (same m/z within ppm **and** same 1/K0 within 0.01 **and** overlapping RT). Running the min_sample_rate axis backwards should return the same order of magnitude (~130). This is a precision patch, **not** a closer for the 3,857 gap.
