/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:76:  a) MS2 trace detection at low threshold: speXtract runs MassTraceDetection on raw fragment data with noise threshold 10, ms2_min_length 0, min_sample_rate 0.5 — generating 8.5M traces/window. diaTracer works on the summed (m/z × IM) matrix and extracts peaks directly, correlating fragment XICs without a full mass-trace detection+valley-split stage. speXtract's trace stage = 48.7% of window time. Also EPD valley-splitting per trace adds cost (comment at :1033: "Valley-splitting is the dominant serial phase (EPD-off S30 20:46 vs 28:40 for MS2 alone)" — i.e., EPD adds ~38% wall).
/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/kimi.err:172:  Hmm wait, one more check on score stage cost: is the IM-band scan really dominant vs dot products? Fragment support ~510 points?? The comment says up to 510 support; a fragment trace spanning the whole gradient? min_sample_rate 0.5 and min_length 0... A fragment with 510 support points passing RT gate... RT gate is apex-RT within 3 s, so support points fall in a narrow RT range but grid-walk cost = support length regardless (walks ALL support, probing pdense). If avg support ~20-40 pts and ~1,200 surviving candidates/precursor: 39k × 1,200 × 30 = 1.4e9 ops/window vs scan 1.6e10. Scan dominates ~10×. Yes, the linear IM-band scan is the score hotspot. And score = 3,753.6 window-wall ≈ ~30-40k CPU-s. Cutting scan 10× cuts score CPU maybe 7-9× → saves ~25-30k CPU-s ≈ 30% of total CPU. Wall: score window-wall 3,753.6 → maybe ~600-800 → saves ~3,000/8 ≈ 370 s wall ≈ 27% of total wall (1,351 s). Biggest single neutral win. Risk: moderate (index construction per window, memory for index, exactness of ordering). Actually simpler alternative: since fragments are sorted by IM, build per-IM-block RT-sorted sub-index, or simply sort fragments by RT within coarse IM buckets... Fine, proposal stands: bucketed 2D index.
